   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Derivatives of a Function at a Point",
    "stem": "The temperature T(t), in °C, of a liquid is differentiable, where t is measured in minutes. Selected values are shown below; no formula for T is available.\n\nt (minutes): 0 | 4 | 6 | 10\nT(t) (°C): 10 | 22 | 28 | 50\n\nUsing the closest tabulated times on either side of t = 5, which statement correctly reports an estimate of T′(5)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "T′(5) ≈ 4 °C/min.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Distant-data trap: (50 − 10)/(10 − 0) = 4 °C/min uses the endpoints of the table rather than the closest times surrounding 5. It measures change over a wider interval, not the requested local interval. Fix: Identify the closest tabulated time below and above the target before computing the slope."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "T′(5) ≈ 6 °C/min.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Missing-denominator trap: 28 − 22 = 6 is the temperature change, not a rate of change. The elapsed time is 6 − 4 = 2 minutes, so the slope requires division by 2. Fix: Divide the change in temperature by the change in time, and check that the units are °C/min."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "T′(5) ≈ 3 °C/min.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The closest tabulated times surrounding 5 are 4 and 6, giving the local slope estimate (28 − 22)/(6 − 4) = 3 °C/min. This nearby secant slope estimates the derivative; the table does not establish its exact value."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The table proves that T′(5) is exactly 3 °C/min.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Estimate-as-exact trap: the nearby data give a secant slope of 3 °C/min, but finitely many table values do not determine the exact derivative at 5. Differentiability alone does not make that secant slope equal to T′(5). Fix: Use ≈ when reporting a derivative obtained from nearby table values unless additional information establishes exact equality."
     }
    ]
   },
