   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Derivatives of a Function at a Point",
    "stem": "A cup of water is heated, and its temperature T(t), in degrees Celsius, is measured at selected times t, in minutes. The table gives: t = 0, 4, 6, 9, 15 with T(t) = 20, 28, 34, 40, 49, respectively. T is differentiable, and no formula for T is given. Using the data in the table, what is the best estimate of T′(7)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "About 1.93 degrees Celsius per minute",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This uses distant data: (49 − 20)/(15 − 0) = 29/15 is the average rate over the whole 15 minutes. It describes overall behavior, not the local slope near t = 7, so it is a weaker estimate than the bracketing values give. Fix: Before computing, circle the two table entries closest to the target time, one on each side, and use only those."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "0.5 minutes per degree Celsius",
